  <content>
  <section><title>Vertex Form with a Leading Coefficient Besides 1</title>
<para>Write \(2x^2+8x+4\) in <term data-schema-version="1.0" data-store="glossary-tooltip">vertex form (of a quadratic expression)</term>.</para>
<para>Original expression</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Rewrite the expression with 2 as a factor.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Add 4 to complete the square.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Combine like terms so we can see the perfect square, \(x^2+4x+4\).</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Rewrite \(x^2+4x+4\) as a squared expression.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 5</emphasis> - Apply the distributive property to put the expression in vertex form.</para>
<title>Try It: Vertex Form with a Leading Coefficient Besides 1</title>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Write \(4x^2+24x+12\) in vertex form.</para>
  <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Here is how to write the quadratic expression in vertex form:</para>
  <para>Original expression</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Rewrite the expression with 4 as a factor.</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Add 9 to complete the square.</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Combine like terms so we can see the perfect square, \(x^2+6x+9\).</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Rewrite \(x^2+6x+9\) as a squared expression.</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 5</emphasis> - Apply the distributive property to put the expression in vertex form.</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example></section>
</content>
